{0 Footnotes}

Treat footnote labels as first-class anchors within a document: complete them,
jump between reference and definition, list every reference, rename a label, and
report the two ways a footnote can be wrong.

{v
Kantorovich relaxed the problem[^kant], and the relaxation is tight[^kant].

[^kant]: Kantorovich, 1942.
v}

A footnote label is an identifier with a definition and a set of references —
the same shape as a heading slug or a block id, which the server already
handles — but it is {e document-scoped}: [\[^kant\]] in one note has nothing to
do with [\[^kant\]] in another. Every rule below follows from that.

{1 Parsing}

Footnotes are a Cmarkit extension, enabled by [~strict:false], which
{!Oystermark.Parse.of_string} already passes. No parser change is needed. The
AST already carries:

{ul
  {- {b definitions} as [Cmarkit.Block.Ext_footnote_definition] nodes, whose
     label is [Cmarkit.Block.Footnote.label] and whose content is
     [Cmarkit.Block.Footnote.block];}
  {- {b references} as inline links satisfying
     [Cmarkit.Inline.is_footnote_reference], i.e. a label key starting with
     [^].}
}

Both carry [Cmarkit.Meta.textloc] under [~locs:true], which is what every
feature here needs. A shared collector — the footnote analogue of
{!Lsp_lib.Link_collect} — walks a document once and returns definitions and
references with their byte ranges; the features below are views over it.

{b Namespace.} Footnote labels do {e not} join the fragment namespace shared by
heading slugs, caret block ids, and attribute ids
({!page-"feature-diagnostics".duplicate_ids}). They are addressed by a
different syntax, they are not link targets, and a note may legitimately have
both a footnote [\[^kant\]] and a block id [^kant].

{1 Completion}

{2 Trigger}

Completion activates when the cursor sits inside an unclosed [\[^] on the
current line: the text between it and the cursor is the prefix. The
[completionProvider] trigger characters already include [\[]; [^] is added.

{b Precedence.} Wikilink detection runs first, so [\[\[note#^<cursor>\]\]] is
block-id fragment completion ({!page-"feature-completion".fragment_completion})
and not this. The two are distinguished by whether an enclosing [\[\[] was
found, not by the [^] — a caret means "block id" inside wikilink brackets and
"footnote" outside them, and nothing else disambiguates them.

A [\[^] that begins a line and is followed by [\]:] is a {e definition}, not a
reference. Completing there would suggest the label being defined; no
completions are offered on a definition line.

{2 Items}

One item per footnote definition in the current document.

{t
  | Field      | Value |
  |------------|-------|
  | label      | the label including [^], e.g. [^kant] |
  | detail     | the first line of the footnote's content, truncated to 60 characters with an ellipsis |
  | filterText | the label without [^] |
  | insertText | the label without [^], since the user typed it |
  | kind       | [CompletionItemKind.Reference] |
}

[detail] is what makes this worth having: a document with [\[^1\]] through
[\[^9\]] is exactly the document where the label does not tell you which
footnote you want.

Order is by first appearance in the document — reading order, which for
numbered footnotes is also numeric order, and which ["1", "10", "2"]
alphabetical order would scramble.

An {e undefined} label already referenced elsewhere in the document is also
offered, with [detail] ["undefined"]. Referring twice to a footnote you have
not written yet is an ordinary intermediate state, and completing the second
reference keeps the two spellings identical.

{1 Find references}

{!page-"feature-find-references"} gains a footnote activation mode, in addition
to its link and heading/block-id modes.

{ul
  {- {b Cursor on a footnote reference}: every reference to that label in this
     document, the cursor's own included, plus the definition when one exists.}
  {- {b Cursor on a footnote definition label}: every reference to it.}
}

Results are LSP [Location]s in the current document only, each covering the
full byte range of the reference (brackets included) or of the definition
label. They are sorted by range start.

Including the definition among a reference's results is a deliberate departure
from the link mode, which returns referrers only: a footnote has exactly one
definition and it is the thing you most want to reach. Reaching it is also what
{!go_to_definition} is for, and both being available is not a conflict.

{1:go_to_definition Go to definition}

{!page-"feature-go-to-definition"} gains one case: a footnote reference
navigates to its definition in the same document. An undefined label navigates
nowhere and is already diagnosed.

The reverse is not go-to-definition: on a definition, [textDocument/references]
is the request that answers "where is this used".

{1 Diagnostics}

{!page-"feature-diagnostics"} gains two document-scoped checks, both at
severity [Warning] with source ["oystermark"]:

{t
  | Message | Condition | Range |
  |---------|-----------|-------|
  | [undefined footnote: ^kant] | a reference whose label has no definition | the reference |
  | [duplicate footnote definition: ^kant] | a label defined more than once | every definition of it |
}

Every occurrence of a duplicate is reported, matching the treatment of
duplicate anchor ids.

An {e unused} definition — defined, never referenced — is {b not} reported. It
renders as an unreferenced note at the foot of the document, which is a
stylistic matter, and warning about it would fire constantly while a document
is being written.

{1 Rename}

{!page-"feature-rename"} gains footnote labels: renaming from a reference or a
definition rewrites the definition and every reference in the document, and
nothing outside it.

The new name is validated as a label: non-empty, and free of [\[], [\]], and
whitespace. An invalid new name fails the rename with a message rather than
producing a document whose footnotes have silently stopped parsing.

{1 Testing}

{ul
  {- Collection: an expect test over a document with a definition, two
     references, a multi-paragraph footnote body, and a label reused as a block
     id, asserting the collected ranges.}
  {- Trigger precedence: a table over cursor positions covering
     [\[^<cursor>\]], [\[\[note#^<cursor>\]\]], a definition line, and a [^]
     in prose.}
  {- Completion: offered set and ordering for a document with numeric labels
     ordered [1], [10], [2] in the source, plus the undefined-label case.}
  {- References: from a reference, from the definition, and with the cursor on
     a footnote in a document that has two.}
  {- Diagnostics: undefined, duplicate (all occurrences), and unused (no
     diagnostic).}
  {- Rename: {b property} — for a generated valid label, renaming and
     re-parsing yields a document whose footnote structure is isomorphic to the
     original, with every occurrence of the old label replaced.}
}

{1 Limitations}

{ul
  {- {b Document-scoped throughout}. No vault-wide footnote index; a label is
     never resolved across files. This is the semantics, not a shortcut.}
  {- {b Inline footnotes} are not supported — Cmarkit's extension has
     definitions only.}
  {- {b No definition-creating quick fix} for an undefined label. It is a
     natural companion to
     {!page-"feature-codeaction-create-unresolved-link"} and is deliberately
     left out of this spec, which is already five features wide.}
  {- {b Not ordered.} Nothing renumbers footnotes or checks that numeric
     labels appear in order; the renderer assigns display numbers.}
  {- Positions and edits follow {!page-"feature-utf16-positions"}.}
}
